# Then the one-month roadmap

## Week 1 — The system is buildable

Goal:

> TemplarOS exists as a reproducible package/image definition.

Deliver:

* seeds;
* closure;
* overlay;
* metapackages;
* dev tooling;
* CUDA;
* mkosi;
* GRUB decision;
* personal install specification;
* QEMU boot.

At the end of this week, stop counting architecture documents and start counting **booted artifacts**.

---

## Week 2 — The system installs on your machine

Goal:

> wipe/spare disk → TemplarOS → session.

Deliver:

* Btrfs target layout;
* provisioning;
* Secure Boot;
* Ubuntu kernel;
* NVIDIA;
* CUDA;
* network;
* audio;
* browser;
* portal;
* Files;
* terminal;
* suspend;
* external displays.

Use an alternate disk if possible during this phase.

---

## Week 3 — The system survives development

Goal:

> use TemplarOS to develop TemplarOS.

Deliver:

* reliable `templar dev deploy`;
* package rollback;
* root snapshots;
* boot rollback;
* build identity;
* interface/schema gates;
* current/previous builds;
* filesystem index running continuously;
* zero repeated drive scans.

At this point I would begin using it as the main development environment.

---

## Week 4 — Make it boring

This is important.

Do **not** spend week 4 adding clever features.

Spend it trying to make yourself forget you're using your own distro.

Fix:

* suspend failures;
* browser quirks;
* DPI;
* file chooser;
* clipboard;
* USB devices;
* sound switching;
* GPU mode;
* terminal launching;
* MIME opening;
* low disk;
* broken package transaction;
* boot failure;
* index corruption;
* stale file index;
* external monitor;
* shutdown;
* firmware;
* update from an old build.

At the end, rebuild it onto a blank disk from nothing.

If the result reproduces your existing environment, **0.1 exists**.

---

# A useful definition of 0.1 success

I would put this in the repo:

> TemplarOS 0.1 is complete when the reference Intel/RTX 4060 system can be recreated from declared Ubuntu 26.04 inputs and the TemplarOS repository, boot with Secure Boot enabled, run the TemplarOS session, compile the TemplarOS workspace and CUDA programs, browse the web, play common media, search indexed local/removable files, update TemplarOS packages transactionally, roll back one bad userspace build, retain one known-good boot path, and complete a normal workday without requiring another desktop environment.

Notice what isn't in that definition:

* public ISO;
* arbitrary computer;
* security advisory team;
* kernel 7.2;
* mirrors;
* OEM installer;
* five-year support;
* public crash reporting;
* ARM;
* graphical app store.

That's why it is achievable.

---

# How much code should this take?

This is where I'd push back on your **100k–400k lines in a month** estimate.

You may be capable of generating that much with agents.

I don't think you should.

The pack already has about **133k lines of Rust**. If "turn this into a personal distro" adds another 300,000 lines of custom Rust, something has probably gone wrong.

A good Ubuntu derivative should contain surprisingly little distribution-specific code.

A productive month might add something more like:

| Work                          | Rough new code/config/tests |
| ----------------------------- | --------------------------: |
| image/archive/package tooling |                       5–15k |
| provisioning/install          |                       3–10k |
| deployment/recovery hardening |                       5–15k |
| hardware diagnostics          |                        2–8k |
| shared index core             |                      10–25k |
| monitoring/reconciliation     |                       5–15k |
| daily-driver fixes            |                       5–15k |
| tests/fixtures                |                      10–30k |

So perhaps **40–100k high-value new lines**, including extensive tests.

More importantly, thousands of lines should disappear as provisional implementations are consolidated.

For this project:

> **negative LOC is often progress.**

One index replacing three scanners is progress.

One system action manifest replacing three menus is progress.

Ubuntu's boot chain replacing custom Secure Boot infrastructure is progress.

A 500-line provisioner replacing a 20,000-line installer is progress.

---

# What spending years on it would buy you

There *is* a legitimate long-term payoff, but it changes in character.

The first month buys you **a product for yourself**.

Another six months could buy you **a distinctive operating system**.

Several years could buy you **a distribution platform**.

## After several months

You could reasonably own:

* polished live ISO;
* Subiquity integration;
* clean source builds;
* public signed overlay;
* reproducible images;
* robust installer;
* actual deployment rollback;
* custom 7.x kernel lane;
* independent NVIDIA module builds;
* polished shared file index;
* FSearch remote frontend;
* Silvaview integration;
* sophisticated memory/resource management;
* comprehensive hardware diagnostics;
* stronger app identity;
* system-wide semantic project integration.

At that point TemplarOS starts having meaningful platform technology Ubuntu/Lubuntu don't have.

## After several years

The value would not mainly be "lighter Ubuntu."

You could own a coherent alternative desktop platform where:

* application identity is reliable;
* apps are treated as supervised systemd objects;
* resource control is systemic;
* file indexing is shared infrastructure;
* projects are first-class;
* Home is an information/work surface rather than a wallpaper;
* file operations are journalled;
* application associations are centrally coherent;
* system actions are declared once;
* OS updates are transactional;
* kernel/driver sets are qualified;
* every relevant state is visible through one CLI;
* system components have explicit activation/rollback semantics.

That is genuinely interesting OS engineering.

And eventually the Ubuntu base becomes almost incidental: TemplarOS's defining intellectual property lives in the integration layer above it.

---

# TempleOS homage

I would keep this deliberately separate from the distro critical path.

The name **TemplarOS** is actually useful here because it gives you permission to make the project visually and culturally distinctive without pretending its architecture resembles TempleOS.

I would establish:

```text
apps/temple/
    README.md
    common/
    games/
    demos/
    tools/
```

and have one long-term rule:

> Port ideas, not dependencies.

Translate Terry's small games/utilities into ordinary safe Rust applications using your own toolkit/interfaces. Don't create a HolyC runtime inside the OS merely for authenticity.

Some of these could become excellent system demos:

* a tiny graphics game demonstrating your renderer;
* a procedural sound app demonstrating PipeWire;
* a simple file/graphics utility;
* a TempleOS-inspired terminal/demo surface;
* system information tools with the deliberately primitive visual aesthetic.

I'd also keep the homage visibly an homage, rather than implying compatibility or lineage.

But I would assign **zero core distro agents to this today**.

First make the machine boot.

---

# The biggest scope cuts

If you want the simple answer to **"How do I make this good without spending years?"**, these are the decisions that matter most:

1. **Target exactly your laptop.**
2. **Stay an Ubuntu binary derivative.**
3. **Use Ubuntu's kernel initially.**
4. **Use Ubuntu's NVIDIA LRM.**
5. **Use Ubuntu's CUDA packages.**
6. **Use Canonical shim + GRUB for Secure Boot.**
7. **Don't build a graphical installer yet.**
8. **Don't create a public build/archive/security operation.**
9. **Use the already-built local signed BuildSet deployment mechanism.**
10. **Use Btrfs as a cheap safety net.**
11. **Finish the shared file index because it actually differentiates the OS.**
12. **Make browser, terminal, media, files, GPU and suspend flawless before adding general-purpose desktop software.**
13. **Measure lightness by idle work and responsiveness, not package count.**
14. **Delete duplicated infrastructure whenever Ubuntu already does it adequately.**
15. **Do not equate agent-generated LOC with progress.**

Under those constraints, **I think one month to a genuinely usable personal TemplarOS is plausible**.

Not "Ubuntu competitor 1.0."

Not "supportable distro for strangers."

But something stronger than a themed desktop session: an independently composed, installable, recoverable Ubuntu-derived developer OS that you can actually use to build itself.

And that is the right intermediate milestone.

